= Apple Tart
:toc:

This recipe makes one 23 cm apple tart and serves six people. Allow about 30 minutes to prepare the ingredients and pastry, 30 minutes for chilling, and 40 minutes for baking.

== Ingredients

For the pastry you will need:

* 250 g plain flour
* 125 g cold butter
* 50 g caster sugar
* 1 egg
* 1–2 tablespoons cold water

For the filling you will need:

* 5 medium cooking apples
* 40 g caster sugar
* 1 teaspoon ground cinnamon
* 1 tablespoon lemon juice
* 15 g butter

You will also need a 23 cm tart tin, a rolling pin, a mixing bowl, a knife, baking paper and a wire rack.

== Prepare the Pastry

Put the flour and sugar into a large mixing bowl and cut the butter into small pieces. Rub the butter into the flour using your fingertips until the mixture looks like breadcrumbs. Try not to work the mixture too much because the butter should remain cold.

Beat the egg in a small bowl and add it to the flour mixture. Mix everything together until a dough starts to form. If the mixture is too dry, add cold water one tablespoon at a time.

The pastry should be soft but not sticky. Wrap it in cling film and put it in the refrigerator for at least 30 minutes.

If the pastry is difficult to roll after chilling, leave it at room temperature for a few minutes.

== Preparing the Apples

Peel and core the apples and cut them into thin slices. Try to cut the slices to a similar thickness so that they cook evenly.

Put the apples into a bowl and add the sugar, cinnamon and lemon juice. Mix gently until the apples are covered with the mixture.

Cooking apples give the tart a softer filling and a more acidic flavour. Eating apples can also be used if you prefer a sweeter tart.

== Before You Begin

Read through the whole recipe before starting. Make sure that you have all the ingredients and equipment that you need.

The butter should be kept cold while the pastry is being made. If the kitchen is warm, the mixing bowl can be put in the refrigerator for a few minutes before you start.

The apples can be prepared while the pastry is chilling.

== Prepare the Tart Tin

Grease the tart tin lightly with butter. A circle of baking paper can be placed in the bottom of the tin to make the tart easier to remove.

== Assemble the Tart

Take the pastry from the refrigerator and roll it out on a lightly floured surface. Roll the pastry into a circle that is slightly larger than the tart tin.

Carefully place the pastry into the tin and press it gently into the bottom and sides. Trim off any pastry that hangs over the edge.

Prick the bottom of the pastry several times with a fork. Arrange the apple slices on top of the pastry in an even layer.

Dot the butter over the apples and sprinkle with a little extra sugar if required. If you have some pastry left over, you can use it to make small decorations for the top.

== Bake the Tart

The oven should be preheated to 190°C before baking.

Put the tart onto the middle shelf and bake for around 40 minutes or until the pastry is golden brown and the apples are soft.

If the pastry starts to become too brown, cover the tart loosely with aluminium foil for the rest of the baking time.

If the pastry is golden but the apples are still firm, the tart should be covered with foil and baked for a little longer. Different types of apples can take different amounts of time to cook.

== Cooling and Serving

Remove the tart from the oven and leave it in the tin for about 15 minutes. This gives the filling time to settle and makes the tart easier to cut.

The tart can be served warm or at room temperature. It can be served with cream, custard or vanilla ice cream if you like.

If you are serving the tart later, transfer it to a wire rack and allow it to cool completely.

== Storing the Tart

The tart can be kept at room temperature on the day it is made. Leftovers should be covered and put in the refrigerator if they will not be eaten within a day.

Bring the tart to room temperature before serving if it has been refrigerated.

== Troubleshooting

If the pastry cracks while rolling it, leave it at room temperature for a few minutes before trying again. The pastry can be easier to work with when it is slightly softer.

If the pastry shrinks during baking, it may have been stretched when it was placed into the tin. Chilling the pastry before baking can help to prevent this.

If the apples are still firm when the pastry is golden brown, cover the tart with foil and continue baking until the apples are soft.
